Step 2. Conditions for each answer class:

10 different conditions for class "0" (Does NOT read academic books most of the time):

- Department is in [BBA, English, JMC, Others]
- Reason for reading books is in [Entertainment Purpose, Keep me informative, To increase my English vocabulary knowledge]
- Amount of time spent reading books per day < 1
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [nan, 1-5]
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [nan, 1-5]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Fiction, Others]
- Frequency of reading books is in [A few times a week, Occasionally, Rarely]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is not "Increase Academic Performance"
- How to manage books for reading is in [Borrow, Others]
- Format that you used for reading books is in [E-books, Printed only]

10 different conditions for class "1" (Reads academic books most of the time):

- Department is in [CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, CIS, Textile, MCT]
- Reason for reading books is "Academic Purpose"
- Amount of time spent reading books per day >= 1
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Non-Fiction, Biography, Politics]
- Frequency of reading books is in [On everyday basis, Several times a week]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is "Increase Academic Performance"
- How to manage books for reading is in [E-books, Buy, Used-all]
- Format that you used for reading books is "Both" (E-books and Printed)

---

These conditions reflect common sense and the example given, where a CSE student reading daily for vocabulary improvement and reading both e-books and printed books answered "1" (yes).

---DIVIDER---

Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Gender: Gender itself may not directly influence whether a student reads academic books most of the time.
- Age: Older students might have more academic responsibilities, possibly reading academic books more often.
- Department: Students in technical or science departments (CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, etc.) are more likely to read academic books most of the time compared to those in non-technical departments.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More time spent reading books per day likely correlates with reading academic books most of the time.
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," it strongly indicates reading academic books most of the time.
- How to manage books for reading: Method may not strongly affect academic reading frequency but E-books or Borrow might be common among academic readers.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher number indicates more engagement with reading, possibly academic.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Higher number indicates more engagement with reading, possibly academic.
- Favorite books for leisure time: If favorite books are Fiction or Biography, less likely reading academic books most of the time; if Non-Fiction or Politics, possibly more academic reading.
- What is the change felt after reading books: If "Increase Academic Performance," likely reading academic books most of the time.
- Who influenced you to read books: If influenced by Teachers, more likely academic reading.
- Which language books do you like to read: If English or academic language, more likely academic reading.
- Where do you read printed books: Location may not strongly affect academic reading frequency.
- Do you read Newspaper: Possibly irrelevant to academic book reading.
- The format used for reading books: E-books might be common for academic reading.
- Frequency of reading books: Higher frequency (everyday or almost everyday) indicates academic reading.

---

